WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.200
How did Michael Corleone go from an&nbsp;
honorable Marine who wanted nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.760
to do with his family's business to a ruthless&nbsp;
Mafia don who ordered a hit on his own brother?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.615
His dramatic transformation is&nbsp;
nothing short of remarkable.
 &nbsp;

00:00:12.615 --> 00:00:16.800
When "The Godfather" begins, we meet the&nbsp;
Corleones, a highly respected and feared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:22.080
mob family run by Don Vito Corleone. Vito deals&nbsp;
with some mob-related business while a gigantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.820
wedding for his daughter is going on outside. We&nbsp;
are also first introduced to Michael Corleone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:30.000
just back from the Marines, as&nbsp;
well as his girlfriend Kay Adams.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.960
In these early scenes, we get a clear sense&nbsp;
that Michael has yet to be corrupted by his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.580
family's shady and often violent dealings.&nbsp;
He makes this especially clear to Kay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.812
who's troubled when Michael pulls back the curtain&nbsp;
ever so slightly on his family's line of work.

00:00:43.812 --> 00:00:45.945
"That's my family, Kay. It's not me."

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:50.100
This is Michael at his most innocent, a far&nbsp;
cry from his state at not just the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.473
first film, but the trilogy as a whole.

00:00:52.473 --> 00:00:54.180
After not playing ball with the head 

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.180
of another mafia family, Vito is&nbsp;
shot in the middle of the street,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:02.925
forcing his hospitalization. Michael discovers his&nbsp;
father's state after seeing a newspaper headline.

00:01:02.925 --> 00:01:06.360
Michael quickly dashes to the hospital&nbsp;
where his father is being looked after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:11.520
only to discover that the entire place has been&nbsp;
cleared out. With no guards or any kind of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.485
Vito is helpless and extremely vulnerable to any&nbsp;
manner of follow-up attacks from his enemies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.485 --> 00:01:21.120
Michael quickly realizes this and springs into&nbsp;
action, convincing a lone nurse to help him move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.160
Vito into another room. He's then greeted by Enzo,&nbsp;
a friend of the family whom his father helped in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:31.365
the past. Enzo is visiting to check in on Vito.&nbsp;
After sending Enzo outside to pose as a guard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.365 --> 00:01:35.994
Michael shares a tender moment with Vito,&nbsp;
telling his father that he's here to protect him.

00:01:35.994 --> 00:01:41.220
"I'm with you now. I'm with you."

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.940
Shortly thereafter, Michael is&nbsp;
confronted outside by Mark McCluskey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.260
a corrupt police captain, as well as a&nbsp;
few of his men. After some heated words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:54.300
Michael is severely beaten by the police until&nbsp;
Tom Hagen and some of Vito's associates arrive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.920
and de-escalate the situation. It's not&nbsp;
only masterfully suspenseful but gives us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.780
just a small taste of how Michael can quickly&nbsp;
take control of the situation in his own way.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:08.220
After the hospital incident, Michael finds&nbsp;
himself dragged into his family's mafia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.180
activity. Michael's heated interaction&nbsp;
with McCluskey has led to some tension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.800
between the Corleones and drug kingpin&nbsp;
Virgil Sollozzo. This is because Sollozzo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.186
ordered the hit on Don Corleone,&nbsp;
and McCluskey is on his payroll.

00:02:20.186 --> 00:02:24.720
Sollozzo seemingly wants to smooth the situation&nbsp;
over, but Michael's older brother Sonny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.385
the acting Don, suspects something sinister.&nbsp;
Looking to take matters into his own hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.385 --> 00:02:33.600
Michael pitches the idea of meeting with&nbsp;
Sollozzo and McCluskey for a sit down where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.220
he would kill them. Tom Hagen, the family's&nbsp;
lawyer, advises against this as the fallout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.792
could reignite a war between the various mob&nbsp;
families. Despite Hagen's recommendations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.792 --> 00:02:47.420
Sonny opts to approve Michael's idea and&nbsp;
begins helping him in planning out the hit.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:50.960
"It's not personal Sonny, it's strictly business."

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.960
The night eventually arrives and Michael,&nbsp;
after fishing a hidden gun out of the bathroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.500
kills both men in the middle of a&nbsp;
restaurant. Not only is it one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.280
the film's most brutally violent scenes —&nbsp;
it also shows that Michael's descent into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.660
darkness has officially begun. The innocent&nbsp;
Marine we met in the film's opening scenes is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:11.015
slowly but surely accepting his role within&nbsp;
his family's dark, destructive dealings.
 &nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.840
Following the murders of Sollozzo and McCluskey,&nbsp;
Michael's family wisely gets him out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.740
country for a while to avoid the fallout.&nbsp;
To ensure his protection, Michael is sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:24.792
to live in Sicily under the watch and protection&nbsp;
of Don Tommasino, an ally of the Corleone family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.792 --> 00:03:28.860
It's here Michael gets a little reprieve to&nbsp;
enjoy life, which isn't difficult among the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:34.020
landscapes of Sicily. He also falls in love with&nbsp;
a local woman named Apollonia Vitelli. Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:38.560
eventually marries Apollonia with the intention&nbsp;
of spending the rest of his life with her.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.180
Meanwhile, back in America, Sonny is&nbsp;
violently gunned down by a siege of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.800
gangsters at a toll booth. Michael is informed&nbsp;
of his brother's death and ends his time abroad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.600
seeking to return to America with his new&nbsp;
wife. However, just before they depart Sicily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:55.560
a car bomb intended for Michael goes off&nbsp;
with Apollonia still inside the vehicle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:00.120
killing her. For the first of what will be many&nbsp;
times, Michael's dealings with the family business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:04.740
have cost him something he loves. This is a key&nbsp;
moment for Michael's still-ongoing transformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:08.981
as the death of his wife ignites his&nbsp;
role as the head of the Corleone mafia.

00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:12.753
"Then be a friend to Michael. Do as he says."

00:04:12.753 --> 00:04:15.258
After his return to America, Michael reunites 

00:04:15.258 --> 00:04:19.860
with Kay and marries her. They have two children&nbsp;
named Anthony and Mary. It's also during this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:24.060
time that Michael steps in as the new Don.&nbsp;
Michael's transformation really reaches its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:27.720
apex as he's now more than willing to&nbsp;
crank up his ruthlessness when needed.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:31.620
This final transformation is cemented&nbsp;
during the baptism scene. It's here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.980
that Michael takes control of the&nbsp;
Corleone mob. Prior to his death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.240
Vito tells Michael that Don Emilio Barzini,&nbsp;
the man responsible for Sonny's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:43.800
will be coming for Michael next. This prophecy&nbsp;
is soon revealed to be accurate. Michael takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:48.604
action in the most brutal way possible — he&nbsp;
wipes out the heads of all five mafia families.

00:04:48.604 --> 00:04:51.480
As Michael attends the baptism&nbsp;
of his sister Connie's child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:55.740
we're treated to a brutal sequence of the&nbsp;
five heads being taken out in gruesome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:59.400
fashion. Not only is the sequence a&nbsp;
testament to Francis Ford Coppola's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.082
stellar direction but it effectively cements&nbsp;
Michael's dark descent into a life of crime.
 &nbsp;

00:05:04.082 --> 00:05:08.100
Michael's life as the Don becomes increasingly&nbsp;
difficult in the events of "The Godfather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.240
Part II {two}," set three years after the&nbsp;
first film. When we're reintroduced to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:17.190
Corleone family amidst yet another large&nbsp;
gathering, Michael is firmly set as Don.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:21.030
"My offer is this: nothing."

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:26.460
The kind and bright-eyed young man from the first&nbsp;
film has already become a distant memory, replaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:31.200
by an eerily broken person. However, there's a&nbsp;
sense that Michael is becoming increasingly more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.089
disillusioned with his role, even though&nbsp;
he's forced to keep up a dominant facade.

00:05:35.089 --> 00:05:38.160
Things get knocked for a serious loop&nbsp;
when, much like his father before him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.780
Michael's life is nearly lost in a mob hit.&nbsp;
Late one night, a barrage of gunshots tears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:47.160
apart Michael's home. Almost immediately, Tom&nbsp;
Hagen concludes that the only way this could've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:51.240
happened is if someone within the family&nbsp;
was secretly in on it. As a result, Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:56.194
is a bit more paranoid and on edge, another&nbsp;
factor affecting his crumbling mental state.
 &nbsp;

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:59.640
In "The Godfather Part II," Michael&nbsp;
tells his older brother Fredo:

00:06:02.063 --> 00:06:06.127
"I know it was you, Fredo. You broke my heart."

00:06:06.127 --> 00:06:10.082
He says this after uncovering Fredo's&nbsp;
true level of allegiance to the family.

00:06:10.082 --> 00:06:14.400
It isn't until New Year's Eve while he's doing&nbsp;
business in Cuba that Michael finally figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:18.480
out that it was Fredo who sold him out. It&nbsp;
becomes clear that Fredo knows Johnny Ola,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.400
the right-hand man of mob boss Hyman Roth. Roth&nbsp;
is the likely suspect for who organized the hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:28.200
on Michael. After this discovery, things are&nbsp;
irreparably damaged between Michael and Fredo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.920
with Fredo believing he should've been&nbsp;
the one to take over when Vito died.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:35.089
"I'm your older brother Mike&nbsp;
and I was stepped over."

00:06:35.089 --> 00:06:38.100
Michael banishes Fredo from the&nbsp;
family but says that he won't be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:42.000
harmed as long as their mother Carmela&nbsp;
is alive. Carmela eventually passes on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.480
which serves as a cue for Michael to begin&nbsp;
tying off loose ends, including Fredo.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:51.340
"I can handle things. I'm&nbsp;
smart! Not like everybody says."

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:54.420
Despite Connie doing her best to&nbsp;
convince him to forgive Fredo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:58.740
his time is officially up. One day while&nbsp;
out fishing, Fredo is shot in the head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:02.514
and dumped into the water, all while&nbsp;
Michael looks on with a somber glare.

00:07:02.514 --> 00:07:05.940
What's important to note about "The&nbsp;
Godfather Part II" is that it's telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:11.340
two stories intertwined – Vito's rise to power&nbsp;
and Michael's fall from grace. The former is set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:16.800
in the early-1900s and tells the story of a young&nbsp;
Vito Corleone working his way up the mafia ladder.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.240
As we see Vito steadily and violently build&nbsp;
what will one day be the Corleone crime family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:25.140
we have a front-row seat to see it all&nbsp;
burn down. By the time the credits roll,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:30.000
Michael has lost his wife and set up the murder&nbsp;
of Fredo. So not only is Michael's marriage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.260
finished but his entire immediate family are all&nbsp;
dead. The last we see of Michael in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:38.760
he sits alone on a bench, staring out&nbsp;
into space. Though judging by the music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:42.600
and Michael's thousand-yard stare, we&nbsp;
can safely assume he's wondering just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:46.500
how everything could go so wrong. This&nbsp;
somber conclusion is the first taste of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.651
the more mentally withered Michael we end up&nbsp;
seeing in "The Godfather Part III {three}."

00:07:50.651 --> 00:07:54.900
When we catch up with Michael at the beginning&nbsp;
of "The Godfather Part III," again at another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:59.460
family gathering, he's firmly in his 60s.&nbsp;
Long gone is the hopeful young guy we met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:03.202
back in the first film, replaced by&nbsp;
a worn-out man with ample regrets.

00:08:03.202 --> 00:08:06.060
One of the many subplots active&nbsp;
in the film involves Sonny's son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:11.012
Vincent, becoming romantically involved with&nbsp;
Michael's daughter Mary. Creepy romance aside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.012 --> 00:08:15.000
this development results in Michael naming Vincent&nbsp;
as the new Don after he chooses to step down.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:19.500
"You won't be able to go back. You'll be like me."

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:21.050
"Good."

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:24.960
You can't talk about "The Godfather Part III"&nbsp;
without talking about Michael's daughter Mary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.100
Corleone. It's very clear that Michael&nbsp;
is still very protective of Mary. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:33.060
is why he tries keeping her and Vincent&nbsp;
apart, due to Vincent's organized crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:36.780
affiliations. Little did Michael know&nbsp;
just how well-founded his fears were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:41.220
especially when the film's shocking climax&nbsp;
rolls around. After a night at the opera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.712
yet another attempt on Michael's life&nbsp;
is made on the stairs of the building.

00:08:44.712 --> 00:08:49.440
Following some gunshots, Michael is wounded. He&nbsp;
then looks over to see Mary, who's been hit point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:54.240
blank in the stomach. We're then treated to a&nbsp;
tragic image of Mary's parents cradling her as she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:58.920
dies, with Michael letting out a blood-curdling&nbsp;
scream. Despite his best attempts to walk away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:03.600
it seems the powers that be needed to take&nbsp;
one last thing from Michael — his daughter.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.500
"Just when I thought I was&nbsp;
out, they pull me back in."

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.940
With one of his own children now dead because&nbsp;
of his own past sins and decisions, Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:17.160
has truly become a broken man. However, when it&nbsp;
comes to the film's final image of Michael, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:22.140
is where things get a bit interesting. In 2020,&nbsp;
"The Godfather Part III" was recut to better fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:27.064
the vision that Coppola originally envisioned —&nbsp;
with resequenced scenes and altered musical cues.

00:09:27.064 --> 00:09:31.200
One such change appears during the ending, which&nbsp;
originally sees a frail and gaunt-looking Michael,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.160
clearly at the end of his life. In that&nbsp;
original scene, we see Michael all alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:40.240
succumbing to his old age and falling out of his&nbsp;
chair, similar to how Vito died in the first film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:44.640
In the recut version, however, the film instead&nbsp;
fades from old Michael to a quote that reads:

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:49.740
"When the Sicilians wish you 'Cent'anni', it means&nbsp;
'for long life'...and a Sicilian never forgets."

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:52.980
Michael has indeed been blessed&nbsp;
with a long life. But when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:55.942
consider how much he's lost, it&nbsp;
might just have been a curse.

